From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ricktav <82380971+ricktav@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 15:43:37 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: s5l8960x-j85: Add Lightning charging

iBoot leaves the D2089's input-current limit at 100 mA, so the iPad mini 2
drains on a cable. Describe what charging needs, from the J85 ADT:

- the D2089's PMU events: the ADT's pmu node, an interrupt controller on
  AP GPIO 21 (active low). All 21 PMIC GPIO lines stay reserved; the A7
  Wi-Fi transport drives line 3 itself;
- the Tristar (CBTL1610) at i2c0 0x1a (i2c0/tristar);
- the Lightning charger on the D2089, at the D2207's addresses as iOS
  12.5.8 AppleD2089PMU uses them, with the PMU line (112) and ADC done
  (89), and the ADT charger node's usb-input-limit-max of 0x960 =
  2400 mA as the board maximum, which the driver holds to 1500 mA;
- the USB role link and usb-psy-name, so the host's allowance reaches
  the charger;
- the battery gauge on UART5 over HDQ (uart5/gas-gauge, TX on GPIO 44).
  The ADT says bq27540; the iPad Air's and the iPad Air 2's, listed the
  same way, answer as a BQ27545. The charger ramps only inside the pack
  limits the gauge reports.

The Tristar's own interrupt (GPIO 116) has no consumer, and the
Lightning accessory policy is not built for this platform. This board
only: the J86 and J87 ADTs were not examined.

On a J85 (installed system), the gauge reports capacity, voltage and
current, and plug and unplug are seen every time. A Mac's USB-C port is
identified as a CDP and a 30 W charger on a USB-C to Lightning cable as
a USB-C source; both get 1500 mA, and the battery charges at +958 mA
and +816 mA. The same charger on a USB-A cable is identified as a DCP.

Assisted-by: LLM
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/s5l8960x-j85.dts | 93 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 93 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/s5l8960x-j85.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/s5l8960x-j85.dts
index 4c12a01a8..c5561e88f 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/s5l8960x-j85.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/s5l8960x-j85.dts
@@ -29,6 +29,11 @@ touch_sync_active: touch-sync-active-pins {
 	touch_sync_inactive: touch-sync-inactive-pins {
 		pinmux = <APPLE_PINMUX(119, 0)>;
 	};
+
+	/* The battery's HDQ line, from the ADT uart5 function-tx (GPIO 0x2c). */
+	uart5_pins: uart5-pins {
+		pinmux = <APPLE_PINMUX(44, 1)>;
+	};
 };
 
 &{/soc/i2c@20a110000/pmic@3c} {
@@ -43,6 +48,94 @@ touch_core: regulator-touch-core {
 		regulator-name = "touch-core";
 		regulator-always-on;
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * PMU events, as iOS 12.5.8 AppleD2089PMU reads them (the D2207's
+	 * layout): the ADT's pmu node is an interrupt controller on AP GPIO
+	 * 21. No PMIC GPIO line is used through it; the Wi-Fi transport drives
+	 * line 3 itself.
+	 */
+	pmu_gpio: gpio {
+		compatible = "apple,d2089-pmic-gpio";
+		gpio-controller;
+		#gpio-cells = <2>;
+		gpio-reserved-ranges = <0 21>;
+		interrupt-controller;
+		#interrupt-cells = <2>;
+		interrupts-extended = <&pinctrl 21 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW>;
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * Lightning charging, at the D2089's registers as iOS 12.5.8
+	 * AppleD2089PMU uses them: connection and charger state at 0x60,
+	 * the input-current limit at 0x4c0, the ADC at 0x500. The ADT's
+	 * charger node allows 0x960 = 2400 mA of input; the driver holds a
+	 * D2089 to the 1500 mA measured so far.
+	 */
+	tristar_charger: charger@60 {
+		compatible = "apple,j85-tristar-charger", "apple,d2089-tristar-charger";
+		reg = <0x60 0x0d>, <0x4c0 0x01>, <0x500 0x04>;
+		reg-names = "status", "control", "adc";
+		interrupt-parent = <&pmu_gpio>;
+		/*
+		 * The PMU line and ADC done. No source events, as on the iPad
+		 * Air 2: the charger's poll of 0x60 sees plug and unplug.
+		 */
+		interrupts = <112 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING>,
+			     <89 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING>;
+		apple,tristar = <&tristar>;
+		input-current-limit-microamp = <2400000>;
+
+		/* The charger decides when USB data may come on. */
+		port {
+			tristar_charger_usb: endpoint {
+				remote-endpoint = <&usbdev_role>;
+			};
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * The Lightning port controller, from the ADT i2c0/tristar node
+ * (tristar,cbtl1610 at 0x1a). Its interrupt (GPIO 116) has no consumer.
+ */
+&i2c0 {
+	tristar: port-controller@1a {
+		compatible = "nxp,cbtl1610";
+		reg = <0x1a>;
+	};
+};
+
+/* Tell the Lightning charger what the USB host allows. */
+&usbdev {
+	usb-psy-name = "lightning";
+	usb-role-switch;
+
+	port {
+		usbdev_role: endpoint {
+			remote-endpoint = <&tristar_charger_usb>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * The battery gauge, from the ADT uart5/gas-gauge node: HDQ over UART5.
+ * The ADT says bq27540, as on the iPad Air and the iPad Air 2, whose
+ * gauges answer as a BQ27545 (DEVICE_TYPE 0x0545). The charger ramps only
+ * inside the pack limits it reads here.
+ */
+&serial5 {
+	pinctrl-0 = <&uart5_pins>;
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	status = "okay";
+
+	onewire {
+		compatible = "ti,hdq-uart";
+
+		battery: fuel-gauge {
+			compatible = "ti,bq27545-hdq";
+		};
+	};
 };
 
 &touchclk {
